# Entity Framework and Database Migrations

## Migrations — Do Not Touch

- Do **not** create, edit, or delete any file in `NTierTemplate.Data/Migrations/` or `NTierTemplateDbContextModelSnapshot.cs`
- Never create migration files directly — the user generates them with `dotnet ef`

When schema changes require a migration:

1. Add entity properties and DbContext configuration
2. Tell the user to run:

```bash
dotnet ef migrations add <MigrationName> \
  --project NTierTemplate.Data/NTierTemplate.Data.csproj \
  --startup-project NTierTemplate.Api/NTierTemplate.Api.csproj
```

## Entity Configuration

- Create persistence classes in `NTierTemplate.Data` (stored fields only; no computed properties)
- Implement `IEntityTypeConfiguration<T>` on the entity class itself (`Configure` method on the entity); do **not** create separate `*Configuration` classes
- `DbContext.OnModelCreating` may configure Identity junction tables; other entities use `ApplyConfigurationsFromAssembly`
- Every entity must have a `DbSet<T>` property in `NTierTemplateDbContext` (alphabetical order)

## Enums in the Database

- **Do not store C# enums as integers** in the database unless absolutely necessary (performance-critical paths, legacy interop, etc.)
- Persist enum properties as **human-readable strings** — configure `HasConversion<string>()` on the property and set a sensible `HasMaxLength`
- C# has no native string-valued enum type; use a normal `enum` in code with EF string conversion, or a `string` column backed by named constants in code
- Enum member names should read clearly in SQL and admin tools (e.g. `PendingRetry`, not `0`)

```csharp
builder.Property(entity => entity.Status)
    .IsRequired()
    .HasMaxLength(32)
    .HasConversion<string>();
```

## Materialized Views

- Create only the entity class in the Data layer (no DTO in domain, no DAO)
- Materialized views are read-only query targets

## Design-Time

- Use `NTierTemplateDbContextFactory` (reads `dbsettings.json`) for design-time migrations
- Run `scripts/create-settings.sh` to create local settings from `example.*.json` templates, then fill in real values
